## AI-Assisted

## Installs ca-certificates so the pinned https apt sources that the Dockerfile
## COPYs in AFTER this can be fetched at all.
##
## Why this exists: debian:trixie-slim carries only the base system, and
## ca-certificates is Priority: standard, so it is absent. Without a trust store
## apt cannot talk https, which would leave the choice between an http downgrade
## for the pinned snapshot and no pin. Neither is acceptable, so the trust store
## is bootstrapped first.
##
## This one apt-get runs against the BASE IMAGE's own sources, which are the live
## archive -- the only unpinned fetch in the image build. That is a deliberate,
## bounded exception:
##   - ca-certificates is build-host tooling. It installs no library the built
##     image links against and is not a build input, so it cannot move a package
##     version in the product.
##   - The pinned snapshot sources land immediately after this, and
##     derivative-maker-docker-setup installs ca-certificates AGAIN from that
##     snapshot, so the finished image carries the pinned version, not this one.
##     This step only has to be good enough to establish TLS.
##
## Deliberately NOT 'apt-get upgrade': the goal is a trust store, not a moving
## base image.

set -x
set -o errexit
set -o nounset
set -o pipefail
set -o errtrace
shopt -s inherit_errexit
shopt -s shift_verbose

apt-get update

DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive \
   apt-get install \
      --no-install-recommends \
      --yes \
      ca-certificates

## Fail loud rather than let the https sources fail later with a confusing
## certificate error: the bundle is the whole point of this script.
if [ ! -s /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt ]; then
   printf '%s\n' "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}: ERROR: /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt missing or empty after install." >&2
   exit 1
fi

apt-get clean
